<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    OstrichChallenge: REGISTERING FRILIOTH AS AN NPC

    WHY THIS FILE HAS TO EXIST. The entity class carries NPCID="traderfrilioth", and that is not a
    label, it is a dictionary key. EntityNPC.NPCInfo does a straight lookup:

        if (this.npcID != "") return npcInfoList[this.npcID];
        return null;

    with no TryGetValue and no fallback, so an unregistered id throws the moment anything asks:

        KeyNotFoundException: The given key 'traderfrilioth' was not present in the dictionary.
        at EntityNPC.get_NPCInfo ... at EntityTrader.PostInit

    AND CLEARING NPCID WOULD NOT HELP, WHICH WAS THE FIRST THING I CHECKED. PostInit guards the
    lookup once, at IL_0027, then uses it again unguarded on its last line:

        emodel.avatarController.SetArchetypeStance(this.NPCInfo.CurrentStance);

    So an empty NPCID swaps a KeyNotFoundException for a NullReferenceException four instructions
    from the end. Every EntityTrader needs a real entry here; the vanilla six all have one.

    THE ROOT IS <npc>, NOT <npcs>, WHICH IS WORTH KNOWING IF THIS EVER LOOKS WRONG. The loader's
    own error string says "No element <npcs> found!", and it is simply wrong: NPCsFromXml.ParseNode
    never checks the root's name at all, it just walks the children looking for npc_info,
    voice_sets and factions. The real vanilla root is <npc>. An xpath of /npcs matches nothing and
    fails silently.

    EVERY VALUE BELOW IS COPIED FROM A VANILLA ENTRY, NOT INVENTED. Sources for each, in order:

      faction="whiteriver"   All five real traders use whiteriver, not trader. Note that the
                             entity class still says Faction="trader", copied from
                             npcTraderTemplate. That split is vanilla's own, not a mistake here:
                             the two fields feed different systems.
      stance="Like"          Joel, Jen, Bob, traderTest and the junk drone are all Like. Hugh is
                             Neutral and Rekt is Dislike, which is clearly characterisation.
      trader_id="11"         His own, added in v0.80.0. See the note below.
      dialog_id="trader"     All five real traders use it. This is what opens the trade window.
      voice_set="trader"     The generic set the junk drone uses, rather than borrowing another
                             trader's character voice. Swap it when Fril records his own.
      portrait="npc_joel"    A PLACEHOLDER, and the most visible one. It is Joel's face in the
                             trade window. The junk drone also points at npc_joel despite being a
                             drone, so vanilla treats this field loosely, but this wants replacing
                             with a portrait of Frilioth before anyone sees it.
      quest_faction="1"      Matches all five. Quests are off in this mod anyway.

    DELIBERATELY OMITTED, each with a vanilla precedent:
      quest_list             Every vanilla value is trader-specific (trader_joel_quests). Pointing
                             at a list that does not exist is worse than having none, and the junk
                             drone omits it too.
      dms_section_type       An enum with a value per named trader. traderTest and the junk drone
                             both omit it.
      name, name_key,        NOT PARSED AT ALL. NPCsFromXml.ParseNPCInfo reads exactly eleven
      greeting_type          attributes and these three are not among them, so vanilla's npc.xml
                             carries them for nothing, the same way entity_class carries Archetype.
                             The displayed name comes from localization_id, which is why there is
                             an npcTraderFrilioth row in Localization.csv.

    >>> trader_id="11", SETTLED IN v0.80.0. He now has his own entry, and it is EMPTY: he is the
    end of the run rather than a shop. The matching <trader_info id="11"> is in our traders.xml
    and the two must always ship together, because TraderData.get_TraderInfo returns
    traderInfoList[TraderID] and an id with no entry hands back a null that later throws.

    TWO CORRECTIONS TO WHAT THIS COMMENT USED TO SAY, both found by reading rather than recalling:

      "10 is the next free one" was WRONG. Vanilla fills ids 1 to 10 inclusive; 10 is a vending
      machine. 11 is the first free one.

      "an out-of-range read" was WRONG. traderInfoList is allocated as
      "ldc.i4 0x100 ; newarr TraderInfo", so it holds 256 slots. An unused id is simply null, and
      the failure is a NullReferenceException further along rather than an index error.

    He keeps his god mode, which is wanted: EntityTrader.PostInit sets IsGodMode inside
    "if (NPCInfo.TraderID > 0)", and 11 is positive. He is the ending, so a stray shot or a
    wandering horde must not be able to end the run by killing him.

    Until v0.80.0 this said trader_id="1", which shared Joel's tables. That was always a
    placeholder, and it meant a survivor who reached Frilioth was offered Joel's entire stock.
-->
<configs>

    <append xpath="/npc">
        <npc_info
            id="traderfrilioth"
            faction="whiteriver"
            stance="Like"
            voice_set="trader"
            portrait="npc_joel"
            trader_id="11"
            dialog_id="trader"
            quest_faction="1"
            localization_id="npcTraderFrilioth"
        />
    </append>

</configs>
